  "_doc": "Brahmic scripts: consonants carry an inherent vowel that a vowel sign replaces and a virama cancels. The text keeps its surface in the primary fold; readings are alternates. Letters are read from their Unicode names: `consonants` spells a consonant name without its inherent vowel (KHA reads kh), `vowels` spells vowel letters and signs, and `qualifiers` are words to drop from a name. `blocks` lists the scripts read and how each reads: `north` drops the inherent vowel where Hindi does (kamla, not kamala), `bengali` also reads the anusvara ng (bangla), `south` reads a word-final anusvara m (malayalam), `khmer` drops the inherent vowel of a word's last consonant (khmer), `plain` keeps every inherent vowel. `vowel_letters` are the letter names that name a vowel. Sinhala names do not spell sounds, so `sinhala` lists its letters. `clusters` respells a consonant pair joined by a virama.",
